import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.30 Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.30 Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living","xkChars":0,"xkWords":572,"xkLessons":{"D.30":{"title":"D.30 Ecology, Resources, Commons, and Sustainable Living","lead":"Verify material constraints, conditions of access, rights, and recovery endpoints separately. A combination crossing a threshold does not automatically prove statistically significant interaction or recovery of an entire system.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5087–5104, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-30#section-d-30. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘Conservation accounting does not provide a unique allocation of value.’ Natural processes, institutions, knowledge, markets, and life connect through specific interfaces. Social choices can change resource use and the distribution of risk, but definitions cannot cancel hydrological and material constraints. Retain domain distinctions among resource systems, units, users, rules in use, and outcomes. [T-main]","Forest cover, species, access to fuel, and household life are not interchangeable. Food stocks, supplies organizations can mobilize, and households' actual access also differ. Pages 433–435 of Sen 1981 distinguish aggregate food availability from the commodity bundles through which individuals can obtain enough food. Aggregate shortages can affect individual hunger, but aggregate quantities alone do not show what each person can obtain. Entitlement sets also relate to initial endowments, exchange or production opportunities, and institutional transfers such as social security. Footnote 5 on page 434 explicitly states that entitlement is used descriptively here, without directly determining which distribution is just. Supply, access, arrival of relief, and nutritional and disease outcomes therefore require separate evidence. [T-main; P-SEN1981]","The exclusion costs and subtractability of common-pool resources are not a legal status of common ownership. Check rights of access, withdrawal, management, exclusion, and alienation separately. Collective organization and engineering support can complement one another, and local rules are not inherently fair. Examine who can participate and who bears costs; the name ‘commons’ cannot supply specific rights. [T-main]","Energy transitions must distinguish continuous fuel flows, construction and in-use stocks, mineral uses, refining quality, and comparable services. Material conservation constrains possibilities without automatically allocating value or responsibility. GDP, material flows, living conditions, care, and ecological outcomes can move in different directions; do not force them into a single overall health score. [T-main]","Water withdrawal, net consumption, evapotranspiration, return flows, recharge, and water levels differ. Area expansion after improved field efficiency can allow local water savings to coexist with higher total net consumption. Better information does not guarantee overall outcomes unless it enters withdrawal constraints and maintenance. Proximity to pollution sources alone does not identify individual health effects either; corresponding exposure and causal evidence are needed. [T-main]","Distribution, recognition, procedure, and remedy in environmental justice each require evidence. Historical contribution, current capacity, future risk, and adaptation needs in climate issues also differ. State measurement, mechanism, scenario, and normative uncertainties separately; no single dimension directly determines compensation shares. [T-main]","Paragraphs 414–415 of the International Court of Justice's July 23, 2025 advisory opinion explain that Article 8 of the Paris Agreement provides cooperative and facilitative support, not a basis for liability and compensation, but does not constitute an express lex specialis excluding general state responsibility. Paragraphs 433–435 distinguish establishing responsibility from the causal showing needed for reparation for particular damage. Multiple causes do not automatically exempt responsibility, but causation cannot be presumed either. [P-ICJ]","These paragraphs do not set an amount that a country owes a person, and scientific findings do not directly confer authority to act. Reparation retains its broader sense of repair and compensation, not only money. Cooperative support, legal responsibility, and claims for particular damage each require their own legal sources, objects, and conditions. A limitation on one mechanism cannot invalidate every other basis. [T-main][P-ICJ]","Actual ecological research reference: Marshall et al.'s 2013 paper manipulates fencing and simulated beaver dams at four sites, measuring willow height and biomass. After ten years, mean height under the combined treatment was 248 cm, exceeding a 2 m threshold; fencing alone gave 160 cm, dams alone 174 cm, and controls 117 cm. The actual manipulation was not randomized wolf reintroduction. [P-MARSHALL]","In Figure 2 and the results, predictive intervals for the interaction effects on both height and biomass cross zero. The combined treatment crossing a threshold therefore cannot alone be called statistically significant synergy. The discussion also acknowledges that dams may alter both water and nutrients, so the effect is not attributed only to water level. A local willow-height target does not equal comprehensive recovery of species, food, water use, and human lives. [P-MARSHALL]","Conservation can simultaneously concern nonhuman individuals, populations, and ecological processes. Scientific knowledge, local knowledge, and legal agency have different qualifications. Successful protection at one position does not automatically offset losses at another. Connections to §2.8, Part V, §6.9, and Parts XII and XIV examine conditions, feedback, aims, responsibility, and disaggregated consequences. [T-main]","The eight entry points are: 30.01, specific social–ecological interfaces; 30.02, rights and governance of commons and common resources; 30.03, flows and stocks in energy and material metabolism; 30.04, access and net consumption in agriculture, food, and water; 30.05, environmental justice and risk distribution; 30.06, climate adaptation and intergenerational responsibility; 30.07, multiple well-being scales in growth and degrowth; and 30.08, conservation, local societies, and nonhuman values. Navigation does not replace all specialist literature and field measurements. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Specify the resource object, actual manipulation, and outcome units.","Distinguish material stocks and flows, household access, nutrition, and living conditions.","Separate powers of access, withdrawal, management, exclusion, and alienation.","List disaggregated outcomes and thresholds before testing interactions and mechanisms.","Separate cooperative support, responsibility, and compensation, preserving gains and losses at different positions."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Water withdrawal","leftText":"Extraction from a source.","rightTitle":"Net consumption","rightText":"Requires accounting for return flows and defining the boundary; it cannot be equated directly with withdrawal."},{"leftTitle":"Common-pool characteristics","leftText":"Exclusion costs and subtractability.","rightTitle":"Legal status of common ownership","rightText":"Specific ownership is a different question."},{"leftTitle":"The combination crosses a threshold","leftText":"A specified outcome reaches a cutoff.","rightTitle":"Significant interaction","rightText":"A separate judgment on a particular statistical scale."},{"leftTitle":"Cooperative support","leftText":"Support provided through an institutional mechanism.","rightTitle":"Compensation for damage","rightText":"Responsibility and specific causation require separate checks."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: expansion of area after improved efficiency can increase total net consumption.","Source boundary: manipulating fencing and hydrological infrastructure cannot be renamed randomized predator reintroduction.","Teaching counterexample: additive effects happen to cross a threshold together without an interaction term."],"questions":["Does the combination meeting a target prove significant synergy?","Do sufficient stocks prove every household has access?","Do multiple causes necessarily exempt responsibility?","Does one ecological improvement offset other losses?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/figures/d30-interface.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 2013 willow-recovery study using fencing and simulated beaver dams","href":"https://sites.warnercnr.colostate.edu/davidcooper/wp-content/uploads/sites/15/2017/02/MarshallHobbsCooper2013RSPB.pdf"},{"title":"Sen 1981: aggregate food availability and individual conditions of access","href":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1882681"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 2013 willow-recovery study using fencing and simulated beaver dams","text":"A factorial manipulation at four sites: after ten years, combined mean height was 248 cm, above 2 m; fencing alone 160 cm, dams alone 174 cm, and controls 117 cm. Predictive intervals for interaction crossed zero.","sourceLabel":"Marshall, Hobbs & Cooper 2013: Stream hydrology limits recovery of riparian ecosystems after wolf reintroduction","sourceUrl":"https://sites.warnercnr.colostate.edu/davidcooper/wp-content/uploads/sites/15/2017/02/MarshallHobbsCooper2013RSPB.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed pages 2–6 (pages 3–7 of this archived PDF): §2 Methods, §3 Results/Figure 2, and §4 Discussion","boundary":"This is not randomized wolf reintroduction, statistically significant synergy, or complete ecosystem recovery. The original data were not recalculated.","href":"https://sites.warnercnr.colostate.edu/davidcooper/wp-content/uploads/sites/15/2017/02/MarshallHobbsCooper2013RSPB.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.30","start_line":5178,"end_line":5195,"startLine":5178,"endLine":5195}],"outputId":"section-d-30","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: less water per mu, more in total","text":"Initially, an artificial 100 mu of land consume 10 units net per mu, totaling 1000. After a change, consumption falls to 8 per mu but the area expands to 150 mu, totaling 1200. Per-mu consumption falls 20% while total net consumption rises 20%; both statements hold simultaneously.","assumptions":"All values use the same net-consumption measure, without treating withdrawals as net consumption; areas are artificially stipulated.","boundary":"This does not explain the causes of real cultivation expansion or water-level changes."},{"title":"Teaching construction: crossing a threshold does not mean interaction","text":"The artificial outcome is y = 1 + 0.6A + 0.6B, with A and B each either 0 or 1. The control is 1, each single treatment 1.6, and the combination 2.2. At a threshold of 2, only the combination qualifies; the interaction difference is 2.2−1.6−1.6+1 = 0. The combination alone can cross a threshold without interaction.","assumptions":"A deterministic additive construction without sampling error; these are not the original willow data.","boundary":"Empirical significance also requires an error model; an artificial zero cannot replace the paper's test."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: expansion of area after improved efficiency can increase total net consumption.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: manipulating fencing and hydrological infrastructure cannot be renamed randomized predator reintroduction.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: additive effects happen to cross a threshold together without an interaction term.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does the combination meeting a target prove significant synergy?","answer":"No. A threshold differs from statistical interaction.","basis":"The central counterexample."},{"question":"Do sufficient stocks prove every household has access?","answer":"No. Access and the arrival of relief require evidence.","basis":"Interfaces."},{"question":"Do multiple causes necessarily exempt responsibility?","answer":"No. Nor can causation of specific damage be directly presumed in the opposite direction.","basis":"The ICJ qualification."},{"question":"Does one ecological improvement offset other losses?","answer":"No automatic offset is warranted; positions and indicators remain separate.","basis":"Boundaries of value judgments."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/figures/d30-interface.png","alt":"Separate supply stocks, organizational mobilization, household access, nutrition, and life.","caption":"Based on D.30."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/figures/d30-ecology.png","alt":"The actual treatment is fencing × dams. The combined value of 248 exceeds 200 without establishing significant interaction.","caption":"The specified scope of the 2013 study."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/figures/d30-math.png","alt":"Net consumption per unit falls while total consumption rises; an additive combination can also be the only treatment to cross a threshold.","caption":"Teaching construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures show material access, the actual ecological manipulation, and two artificial calculations.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["Supplementary material was not read, and the original ecological data were not independently recalculated.","The later historical famine cases, complete formal development, and related data in Sen 1981 remain unread. This section uses only the specified conceptual pages to explain the difference between aggregate availability and individual conditions of access."],"taskId":"S-D-30","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.30, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4999–5016","claim_support":["Multiple scales of resources and ecology, rights, justice, climate responsibility, recovery, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-MARSHALL","title":"Marshall, Hobbs & Cooper 2013: Stream hydrology limits recovery of riparian ecosystems after wolf reintroduction","url":"https://sites.warnercnr.colostate.edu/davidcooper/wp-content/uploads/sites/15/2017/02/MarshallHobbsCooper2013RSPB.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013，Proc R Soc B280:20122977","locator":"Printed pages 2–6 (pages 3–7 of this archived PDF): §2 Methods, §3 Results/Figure 2, and §4 Discussion","claim_support":["Fencing × simulated beaver dam manipulations at four sites. After ten years, the combined mean was 248 cm, above 2 m; fencing alone 160 cm, dams alone 174 cm, and controls 117 cm. Predictive intervals for interaction crossed zero, and dams could alter both water and nutrients."],"limits":["The lesson author read the specified methods, results, discussion, and figure captions, but not supplementary material, and did not recalculate raw data. The account does not claim randomized wolf release, significant synergy, or comprehensive ecological recovery."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-ICJ","title":"ICJ 2025 advisory opinion on climate change, United Nations A/79/979","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=A%2F79%2F979&t=pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Opinion dated 2025-07-23; UN transmission version dated 2025-08-04","locator":"Opinion dated 2025-07-23, §§414–415 (pages 127–128) and 433–435 (pages 132–133)","claim_support":["Article 8 of the Paris Agreement provides cooperative support rather than a basis for compensation, without excluding general state responsibility. Responsibility and proof of causation for reparation of specific damage are separate. Multiple causes do not automatically exempt responsibility, nor can causation be presumed."],"limits":["The lesson author directly read the specified paragraphs and necessary context, not all 142 pages. They are not used to determine a country's compensation share owed to a specific subject. Reparation is not limited to money."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-SEN1981","title":"Amartya Sen: Ingredients of Famine Analysis: Availability and Entitlements","url":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1882681","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Quarterly Journal of Economics 96(3), August 1981, pp.433–464","locator":"Printed pages 433–435, including footnote 5 on page 434; PDF pages 2–4 of the local JSTOR scan, with the bibliographic cover on PDF page 1 also checked","claim_support":["Distinguishes aggregate food availability from commodity bundles through which individuals can obtain enough food. Aggregate shortages can affect individual hunger, but aggregate quantities cannot replace individual conditions of access.","Page 435 connects entitlement sets with initial endowments, exchange or production opportunities, and institutional transfers. Footnote 5 on page 434 explicitly states that entitlement is used descriptively rather than normatively in this article."],"limits":["Only the specified pages 433–435 and bibliographic cover were read. The final sentence on page 435 continues onto the next page; no elaboration is based on that unread continuation.","The three later famine cases and complete formal development were not read, nor were historical data checked. No conclusion is drawn about the causes of an actual famine or an individual's legal rights."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.30 Ecology, Resources, Commons, and Sustainable Living; Chinese source lines 5087–5104; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/source/S-D-30.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-30/diagrams.md","keyTerms":[{"term":"Entitlement (as used in Sen 1981)","text":"The commodity bundles a person can obtain through the legitimate means available in the society at that time, within a given social context. Production, exchange, and institutional transfers may all contribute. Footnote 5 on page 434 explains that this is descriptive rather than normative usage; it does not declare the existing distribution just.","href":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1882681","ref":"Printed pages 433–435"},{"term":"Aggregate food availability and individual conditions of access","text":"The former asks how much food exists overall; the latter asks which bundles sufficient to sustain life a particular person can actually obtain. Aggregate shortage can affect the latter, but the two judgments still require separate inquiry.","href":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1882681","ref":"Printed pages 434–435"}],"editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"b8cd8439bc6c47a7807be841de3f327a49a702ca20705f6cab05ddf803be7fd1","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-30.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-30.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-30.md"};function d(u,e,h,p,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-30" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.30</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-30" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.30 Ecology, Resources, the Commons, and Sustainable Living {#section-d-30}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Verify material constraints, conditions of access, rights, and recovery endpoints separately. A combination crossing a threshold does not automatically prove statistically significant interaction or recovery of an entire system.</span></div><p>In the main text, see §2.8, §5, §6.9, §12, and §14. Research entries cover 30.01–30.08.</p><p>Natural processes, institutional rules, knowledge, markets, and everyday life are connected through specific interfaces. Social choices can change resource use and the distribution of risks. They cannot remove hydrological or material constraints by definition. Resource systems, resource units, appropriators, rules in use, and outcomes call for domain-specific analysis. Forest cover, species, access to fuel, and household life cannot stand in for one another.</p><p>Distinguish food stocks, supplies an organization can requisition, and food households actually obtain (see Sen, 1981). In analyzing a food crisis, assess supply, access, delivery of relief, and consequences for nutrition and disease separately. Total stocks in storage or total imports alone do not establish that everyone’s needs have been met.</p><p>The costs of exclusion and the subtractability of common-pool resources are distinct from joint ownership under law. Consider rights of access, withdrawal, management, exclusion, and transfer separately. Collective organization and engineering support can complement one another. Local rules are not automatically fair. In energy transitions, distinguish continuous fuel flows, construction activity, in-use stocks, uses of minerals, grades of refined materials, and comparable services. Conservation accounting does not yield a unique allocation of value.</p><p>Water withdrawals, net consumption, evapotranspiration, return flows, recharge, and water levels are distinct. Teaching example. The cultivated area expands after field-level efficiency improves. Local water savings need not reduce total net consumption. Better information is also insufficient to guarantee the overall outcome if it does not feed into withdrawal constraints and maintenance practices. Distribution, recognition, procedure, and remedy in environmental justice each require their own evidence. Proximity to a pollution source does not by itself identify individual health effects.</p><p>In discussions of climate, distinguish historical contributions, current capabilities, future risks, and adaptation needs. Uncertainty about measurements, mechanisms, scenarios, and normative matters must also be kept separate. Support through cooperation, determinations of legal responsibility, and compensation for specific harms each depend on the applicable sources of law, objects, and conditions. Limits on one mechanism do not automatically rule out other grounds. Scientific assessments do not directly determine shares of compensation or authority to act. See International Court of Justice (2025, §§414–415, 433–435). GDP, material outcomes, living conditions, care, and ecological outcomes need not be combined. Conservation can concern individual nonhuman beings, populations, and ecological processes at the same time. Scientific knowledge, local knowledge, and legal representation involve distinct qualifications. Success in protecting one position does not automatically offset losses at another.</p><p>For ecological restoration, state the actual manipulation, the measured endpoint, and the target system separately. If the study directly changes fencing and hydrological infrastructure, the treatment cannot be renamed random reintroduction of predators. Even if only the combination reaches a given threshold, that does not by itself establish a statistically significant interaction. The number of species, biomass, water use, access to food, and people’s lives can change in different directions. Keeping the outcomes separate has more explanatory power than combining them into a health score without a basis.</p><p>Research topics. 30.01 Social–ecological systems and human–environment relations. 30.02 Commons, shared resources, and collective governance. 30.03 Energy transitions and material metabolism. 30.04 Agriculture, food, and water resources. 30.05 Environmental justice and the distribution of risk. 30.06 Climate change, adaptation, and intergenerational responsibility. 30.07 Growth, degrowth, and multiple scales of well-being. 30.08 Conservation, local communities, and nonhuman values.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.30"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=a(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
